---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BackToHome from '../../components/BackToHome';
import { buildBreadcrumb } from '../../lib/seo';

const canonicalUrl = 'https://asimali.online/blog/laravel-application-structure/';
const title = 'How I Structure a Laravel App for Maintenance';
const description =
  'A guide to structuring Laravel applications for long-term maintenance: service layers, Eloquent discipline, queues, state machines, and query hygiene.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "@id": `${canonicalUrl}#blogposting`,
    "url": canonicalUrl,
    "headline": title,
    "description": description,
    "datePublished": "2025-06-10",
    "dateModified": "2025-06-10",
    "author": { "@id": "https://asimali.online/#asim-ali" },
    "publisher": { "@id": "https://asimali.online/#asim-ali" },
    "mainEntityOfPage": canonicalUrl,
    "knowsAbout": ["Laravel", "PHP", "Software Architecture", "MySQL", "Redis"]
  },
  buildBreadcrumb('blog/laravel-application-structure', 'How I Structure a Laravel App for Maintenance')
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Asim Ali`}
  description={description}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  type="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <main id="main-content" class="w-full">
    <article class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[820px] px-5 sm:px-8 lg:px-12 pt-24 sm:pt-28 pb-16 flex flex-col">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3 mb-8">
        <a href="/blog" class="text-[13.5px] text-muted hover:text-white transition-colors w-fit font-display">&larr; All posts</a>
        <BackToHome className="mb-0" />
      </div>

      <p class="text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-orange font-semibold mb-4">Laravel · Architecture</p>
      <h1 class="text-[34px] sm:text-[48px] font-bold text-white leading-[108%] tracking-normal font-display">
        How I Structure a Laravel Application for Long-Term Maintenance
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[16px] sm:text-[18px] text-muted leading-[150%] font-display mt-6 max-w-[680px]">
        Almost every Laravel codebase I have inherited was easy to write and
        hard to live in. The features shipped on time in month one and became
        progressively slower, harder to test, and scarier to change by month
        twelve. Structure is what decides which kind of codebase yours becomes.
      </p>
      <time class="text-[13px] text-dim font-display mt-5 block" datetime="2025-06-10">
        June 10, 2025 · 8 min read
      </time>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          The maintenance test
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          I judge a structure by one question: <strong class="text-white">can a developer who has never
          seen this project make a safe change in their first week?</strong> Not add a feature
          with a walkthrough — make a small change and know, before deploying,
          what it affects. Most Laravel applications fail this test not because
          the framework is misused, but because business logic accumulates in
          the wrong places until nobody can say what touching one thing breaks.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Everything below comes from production systems I have built or
          maintained over three years — a territory-driven enterprise CRM, a
          centralized SSO platform, logistics and inventory software, and a
          document-generation platform for telecom construction. The patterns
          are not exotic. They are the boring decisions that pay rent.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          1. Thin controllers, honest service layers
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          The usual failure mode is a controller that validates, authorizes,
          computes, writes, and emails in one method. Refactoring that into
          service classes is standard advice — the part people get wrong is
          making services <em>too</em> general. A service called <code class="text-lime">DealService</code>
          that does validation, pricing,
          notifications, and reporting is a controller with dependency injection.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          What works better: services named after the business operation they
          own. In our CRM the target-allocation logic (cascade revenue targets
          down a reporting chain with double-spend prevention) lives in one
          service whose only job is that calculation. The quote-to-PDF
          lifecycle lives in another. When someone asks "where do we change how
          targets cascade?", the answer is one class, not a search across
          controllers.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Two rules keep the layer honest: services return Eloquent models or
          DTOs rather than arrays with shape-shifting keys, and services never
          read the request object. If a service reaches into
          <code class="text-lime">$request</code>, some controller has leaked its
          transport layer into the domain.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          2. Model discipline without dogma
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Eloquent models are pleasant until they become the dumping ground for
          every operation related to the entity. I let models own what is true
          about them — scopes, accessors, relationship definitions, small
          state-changing methods — and push anything that coordinates across
          entities, talks to external services, or sends mail up into the
          service layer or dedicated action classes.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          The counter-intuitive part is that model bloat is usually a symptom
          of missing objects, not an argument against Eloquent. When a model
          passes 2000 lines, ask what concept nobody named. In the CRM it was
          the reporting hierarchy itself: once TerritoryAccessService and
          TargetHierarchyService owned cycle-safe descendant traversal
          (getDescendantIds), the permission checks, query scopes, and
          visibility rules all shrank to a few lines each.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          3. Authorization as data, not flags
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          The single highest-leverage structural decision in the enterprise
          systems I have built was treating access rules as data in the
          database rather than booleans scattered through the codebase.
          Laravel Gates and Policies give you the enforcement point; the
          structure comes from modeling the substance.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          In a seven-level sales organization, territory grants for companies,
          states, and cities propagate down the reporting chain and have to
          revoke in cascade inside one transaction when a person is removed.
          That is only correct and auditable if the grant chain is recorded as
          data — rows you can query — instead of logic you must simulate. The
          same principle applies to budget chains, approval workflows, and
          entitlements: if a rule needs to change at runtime or be audited
          after the fact, it is data, not code.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          4. Move deferred work to queues on day one, not month nine
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Every application starts with "this will never be slow enough to need
          a queue", and the ones that keep that promise are the ones that put
          the pattern in place early, so the first Excel import of 20,000 rows
          gets dispatched instead of being inlined into a POST request. Our
          queued workflows process bulk imports and document generation with
          retry pipelines and idempotent jobs. The structural benefit came
          earlier: request handling stayed predictable for users and the
          failure surface stayed visible for the team.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          The rule I follow: if the operation reads like a list of things that
          could fail independently (parse, persist, convert, email), each
          independent step belongs in its own queued job with its own retry
          semantics. One giant job that emails at the end is how duplicate
          sends are born.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          5. Multi-stage processes deserve state machines
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Anything with stages — shipments moving from "in transit" to
          "received by warehouse" to "installed", or construction packages
          advancing through pre-construction, construction, and
          post-completion — will collect edge cases like "can I reject a
          document that was revised after approval?" if the states are
          free-text columns. A finite state machine with explicit allowed
          transitions answers those questions by construction. Our logistics
          engine runs a 9-state lifecycle machine and the document pipeline runs a
          3-stage sequential one with rework loops; in both cases the state
          machine is the smallest object in the system and the one I would
          defend hardest.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Bonus: state machines make history free. When every transition is an
          event, the audit trail is a table, not an archaeology project.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          6. Query hygiene is a habit, not a rescue project
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          Keeping our genealogy graph API and enterprise dashboards fast did
          not come from one clever rescue fix. It came from treating N+1 queries,
          per-node permission checks, and un-indexed lookups as
          <strong class="text-white">defects</strong> with the same severity as a
          failing test, every sprint. Concretely: resolving five-tier
          family-tree permissions in memory via a single breadth-first graph
          pass (<code class="text-lime">GraphContextService</code>) instead of
          querying per node, replacing multiple dashboard count queries with
          single <code class="text-lime">selectRaw</code> CASE / SUM aggregates,
          and using tagged cache invalidation on read-heavy endpoints.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          The structural habit is discipline in code review: a pull request
          that loops a collection with queries inside gets the same treatment
          as one that breaks a test. Catch it at review time and "the API got
          slower over six months" never happens.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          7. Tests protect behavior, not percentages
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          I aim coverage at the paths where mistakes are expensive: authorization,
          money (target allocation, billing), state transitions, and
          irreversible operations. The document-generation platform I
          architected carries 60+ automated Pest/PHPUnit feature tests,
          including a dedicated 48-case adversarial authorization suite, with
          static analysis through PHPStan — not for the metric, but because a
          contractually mandated PDF
          format has exactly one correct output and no consumer will report a
          formatting bug politely. Tests earn their maintenance cost where
          failure is silent or hard to reverse.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-[26px] sm:text-[30px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          What this buys you
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          None of these patterns make the first month faster. Most of them cost
          an afternoon each. What they buy is the twelfth month: new features
          that slot into known places, incidents that are diagnosable from
          structure instead of archaeology, and a codebase the next developer
          — or you, six months from now — can work in safely.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[16px] text-muted leading-[170%] font-display mt-4">
          If you want to see how these patterns look inside full production
          systems, explore the
          <a href="/projects/ccms" class="text-orange hover:underline">CCMS Enterprise CRM</a>,
          <a href="/projects/shipment-tracker" class="text-orange hover:underline">Shipment Tracker &amp; IMS</a>,
          <a href="/projects/enterprise-single-sign-on" class="text-orange hover:underline">Enterprise Unified SSO</a>,
          and
          <a href="/projects/close-out-package-system" class="text-orange hover:underline">Close-Out Package (COP) System</a>
          case studies in my portfolio.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-orange/30 bg-orange/8 p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] sm:text-[26px] font-bold text-white leading-[115%] font-display">
          Working on a Laravel codebase that needs structure?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-white/85 leading-[160%] font-display mt-3">
          I take on maintenance, refactoring, and performance work on
          production Laravel applications. Remote, worldwide.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-5">
          <a href="/#contact-form-section" class="rounded-lg bg-cta px-5 py-3 text-[14px] font-semibold text-white hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity">Get in touch</a>
          <a href="/#projects" class="rounded-lg border border-white/30 px-5 py-3 text-[14px] font-semibold text-white hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors">View case studies</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
